github.com/seonho12-54/qodo-coderabbit-benchmark/pull/6251#issuecomment-5534691926","viewerDidAuthor":true},{"id":"IC_kwDOUMmgW88AAAABSeTj8A","author":{"login":"qodo-code-review"},"authorAssociation":"NONE","body":"<h3>Code Review by Qodo</h3>\n\n<code>🐞 Bugs (1)</code>  <code>📘 Rule violations (0)</code>  <code>📜 Skill insights (0)</code>\n\n<img src=\"https://www.qodo.ai/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/light-grey-line.svg\" height=\"10%\" alt=\"Grey Divider\">\n\n<br/>\n\n<img src=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/High-634FD1?style=flat-square\" height=\"20px\" alt=\"Action required\">\n\n<details>\n<summary>  1.  Old overrides evade wrapping <code>🐞 Bug</code> <code>≡ Correctness</code></summary>\n\n<br/>\n\n> <details open>\n><summary>Description</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n><pre>\n><b><i>__init_subclass__</i></b> now treats any second parameter as the new context parameter, so legacy\n>overrides such as <b><i>process_response(self, response)</i></b> are not wrapped. Flask then calls them as\n><b><i>process_response(ctx, response)</i></b>, raising <b><i>TypeError</i></b> during response finalization; exception,\n>finalize, logging, and teardown overrides with legacy parameters are similarly affected.\n></pre>\n></details>\n\n> <details>\n><summary>Code</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n><code>[src/flask/app.py[287]](https://github.com/seonho12-54/qodo-coderabbit-benchmark/pull/6251/files#diff-d3e0349504081bb501c611b3954be364188a4cf442056181272e27e830ae446eR287-R287)</code>\n>\n>```diff\n>+            if param is None:\n>```\n></details>\n\n> <details>\n><summary>Evidence</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n><pre>\n>The class hook explicitly exists to detect old overrides and apply <b><i>remove_ctx</i></b>, which removes a\n>leading <b><i>AppContext</i></b> before invoking the legacy method. The stable public API documents\n><b><i>process_response(response)</i></b>, while the new implementation calls `self.process_response(ctx,\n>response)<b><i>; under the new </i></b>param is None<b><i> condition, the legacy </i></b>response` parameter prevents\n>wrapping and the call receives one positional argument too many.\n></pre>\n>\n> <code>[src/flask/app.py[258-260]](https://github.com/seonho12-54/qodo-coderabbit-benchmark/blob/7043eef6db6b763b3e019fc42ee43e5e846d02a5/src/flask/app.py/#L258-L260)</code>\n> <code>[src/flask/app.py[84-105]](https://github.com/seonho12-54/qodo-coderabbit-benchmark/blob/7043eef6db6b763b3e019fc42ee43e5e846d02a5/src/flask/app.py/#L84-L105)</code>\n> <code>[src/flask/app.py[1011-1032]](https://github.com/seonho12-54/qodo-coderabbit-benchmark/blob/7043eef6db6b763b3e019fc42ee43e5e846d02a5/src/flask/app.py/#L1011-L1032)</code>\n> <code>[src/flask/app.py[1384-1385]](https://github.com/seonho12-54/qodo-coderabbit-benchmark/blob/7043eef6db6b763b3e019fc42ee43e5e846d02a5/src/flask/app.py/#L1384-L1385)</code>\n> <code>🌐 [The stable Flask API documents the overridable method as process_response(response), confirming that a legacy override already has a second parameter after self.](https://flask.palletsprojects.com/en/stable/api/)</code>\n></details>\n\n> <details>\n><summary>Agent prompt</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n>```\n>The issue below was found during a code review. Follow the provided context and guidance below and implement a solution\n>\n>## Issue description\n>The override migration check only detects legacy methods without a second parameter. Legacy methods that already accepted values such as `response`, `e`, or `exc` evade wrapping and receive an unexpected additional `AppContext` argument at runtime.\n>\n>## Issue Context\n>Restore validation that the second parameter actually represents the new context argument, using its `ctx` name or `AppContext` annotation. Preserve support for qualified string annotations and `AppContext` subclasses.\n>\n>## Fix Focus Areas\n>- src/flask/app.py[281-295]\n>```\n> <code>ⓘ Copy this prompt and use it to remediate the issue with your preferred AI generation tools</code>\n></details>\n\n<hr/>\n</details>\n\n\n\n<im